[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 500,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 539,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71211,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 247,
        "headingId": 539,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Glomus tumour is a mesenchymal tumour composed of cells resembling the modified perivascular smooth muscle cells of the glomus body.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T14:48:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 501,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 540,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71212,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 247,
        "headingId": 540,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8711/0 Glomus tumour, NOS</p>\r\n<p>8712/0 Glomangioma</p>\r\n<p>8713/0 Glomangiomyoma</p>\r\n<p>8711/1 Glomangiomatosis</p>\r\n<p>8711/1 Glomus tumour of uncertain malignant potential</p>\r\n<p>8711/3 Malignant glomus tumour</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T14:48:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 502,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 541,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71213,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 247,
        "headingId": 541,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F35 &amp; XH47J2 Benign neoplasm of urinary organs &amp; Glomus tumour, NOS</p>\r\n<p>2C9Y &amp; XH21E6 Other specified malignant neoplasms of urinary tract &amp; Glomus tumour, malignant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T14:48:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 503,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 542,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71214,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 247,
        "headingId": 542,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T14:48:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 504,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 543,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71215,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 247,
        "headingId": 543,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Glomangioma; glomangiomyoma; glomangiomatosis; glomus tumour of uncertain malignant potential; malignant glomus tumour</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T14:48:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 505,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 544,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71216,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 247,
        "headingId": 544,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In the genitourinary tract, glomus tumours primarily occur in the kidney and are extremely rare in the bladder and testis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T14:48:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 506,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 545,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71217,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 247,
        "headingId": 545,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most renal glomus tumours are discovered incidentally. Other presentations include flank pain, haematuria, or a mass lesion {28438616}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T14:48:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 507,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 546,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71218,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 247,
        "headingId": 546,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Renal glomus tumours are rare, arising at any age (mean: 50&nbsp;years) with no sex predilection {31594471}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T14:48:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 508,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 547,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71219,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 247,
        "headingId": 547,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Autosomal dominant inheritance has been reported in the setting of multiple familial glomus tumours due to inactivating mutations or uniparental disomy of the <em>GLMN</em> gene {12383095; 10727310; 10364524}. Biallelic inactivation of <em>NF1</em> in neurofibromatosis type&nbsp;1 has been reported {25426397}.</p>\r\n<p>Besides <em>GLMN</em> and <em>NF1</em> gene mutations, <em>NOTCH2</em> gene rearrangements are seen in as many as 52% of glomus tumours {32604167}. Malignant glomus tumours are more likely to have <em>BRAF</em> p.V600E mutations, a potential therapeutic target {28834810}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T14:48:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 509,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 548,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71220,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 247,
        "headingId": 548,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Because of the absence of glomus bodies in visceral organs, the pathogenesis of visceral glomus tumours remains unknown.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T14:48:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 516,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 555,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71221,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 247,
        "headingId": 555,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumours are small, well-circumscribed lesions with a solid, cystic, haemorrhagic, or spongy cut surface {28438616}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T14:48:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 510,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 549,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71222,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 247,
        "headingId": 549,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Glomus tumours are composed of monotonous small round cells with central round nuclei and well-defined cell borders, lacking marked atypia and mitotic activity, arranged around small vessels. Stromal hyalinization, as well as myxoid, cystic, and symplastic changes, may be present {28698815}. Cavernous haemangioma&ndash;like malformations characterize glomangioma, whereas areas of smooth muscle differentiation are seen in glomangiomyoma.</p>\r\n<p>Immunohistochemistry shows SMA, MSA, h-caldesmon, pericellular collagen&nbsp;IV, and calponin positivity; keratins and melanoma-associated markers are negative. Ultrastructurally, the neoplastic cells contain cytoplasmic filaments compatible with myoid differentiation.</p>\r\n<p>Because of the monotonous epithelioid morphology, a low-grade clear cell renal cell carcinoma may be mimicked, particularly in core needle specimens. The main differential diagnosis is juxtaglomerular cell tumour with associated hypertension; rhomboid-shaped renin crystals on ultrastructural analysis are diagnostic.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T14:48:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 511,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 550,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 71223,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 247,
        "headingId": 550,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-25T14:48:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 512,
